.TH CALLGLANCE 1 "October 2026" "CallGlance" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
callglance \- see at a glance whether your connection is good enough for calls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B callglance
[\fB\-\-background\fR] [\fB\-\-demo\fR] [\fB\-v\fR]
.br
.B callglance
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
CallGlance measures latency, jitter and packet loss to your router, your ISP's first router
and public internet targets, and says in plain words whether your connection is good for
calls, and whether your Wi\-Fi or your ISP is to blame.
.PP
Without a command, it starts the background service (or, if it is already running, shows its
details). On GNOME it shows a coloured dot in the top bar; on other desktops, a tray icon.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-background
Start without showing anything (used at login).
.TP
.B \-\-demo
Simulate a connection, to try the interface.
.TP
.B \-v
More log output; repeat for even more.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBstatus\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-watch\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIN\fR]
Print the verdict and the numbers per segment. Exits with 0 when the connection is good
for calls, 2 otherwise.
.TP
.B speedtest
Download and upload speed, with latency under load and a bufferbloat grade.
.TP
.B doctor
Check what CallGlance can use on this system.
.TP
\fBautostart\fR [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR|\fBstatus\fR]
Start at login, or not.
.TP
\fBextension\fR [\fBinstall\fR|\fBenable\fR|\fBstatus\fR]
Install or enable the GNOME Shell extension.
.TP
.B enable\-icmp
Allow unprivileged ping for the most precise measurements (asks for your password).
.TP
.B quit
Stop the running CallGlance.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/callglance/config.json
Settings.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/callglance/history.sqlite3
The last 24 hours of measurements.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/rafay-ah/callglance
